  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Vedalken are a race of partially-amphibious, blue-skinned humanoids\n\tresembling earless humans in body paint. Introduced in Guildmaster’s Guide to\n\tRavnica, vedalken have a cultural drive to pursue improvement toward\n\tperfection. They tend to be rational and private, and are driven toward\n\tintellectual pursuits like history, medicine, and picking pockets for some\n\treason."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mechanically, the Vedalken is an excellent option if you want to play a\n\tclever spellcaster, but they lack anything to support a class which uses\n\tweapons. Their ability increases are in Intelligence and Wisdom, and no class\n\tcan make use of both (disregarding skills). Beyond that, they match the\n\tGnome’s Gnome Cunning feature, providing significant protection against many\n\tspells, and Partially Amphibious largely removes the need for spells like\n\tWater Breathing. Perhaps most notable is Tireless Precision, which offers a\n\tproficiency in a skill and a tool plus a bonus d4 on the roll whenever you use\n\teither. The skill list is limited, but still contains several excellent\n\toptions, and there’s nothing stopping you\\ from combining it with\n\tExpertise."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The custom origin rules interact strangely with the Vedalken due to their\n\tTireless Precision trait. The rules allow you to pick any skill rather than\n\tthose from the list, but the bonus d4 technically isn’t addressed. As a DM I\n\twould rule that the d4 applies to whatever skill and tool you choose, but be\n\tsure to discuss it with your DM before making assumptions."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
